.TH "BTLE\-EXTCAP\.PY" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBbtle\-extcap\.py\fR \- capture BLE advertising from an Ubertooth in Wireshark
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
btle\-extcap\.py \-\-extcap\-interfaces
btle\-extcap\.py \-\-extcap\-interface=ubertooth<serial> \-\-extcap\-dlts
btle\-extcap\.py \-\-extcap\-interface=ubertooth<serial> \-\-extcap\-config
btle\-extcap\.py \-\-extcap\-interface=ubertooth<serial> \-\-capture \-\-fifo=PATH
[\-\-channel=37|38|39]
btle\-extcap\.py \-V
btle\-extcap\.py \-h
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
btle\-extcap\.py(1) is an external capture program, which lets Wireshark capture BLE advertising packets from an Ubertooth directly, with no capture file in between\. Wireshark calls it; it runs ubertooth\-btle(1) and lets that tool write PCAP data into the fifo Wireshark opened\.
.P
It is not a tool to run by hand in normal use\. Install it where Wireshark looks for extcap programs (see INSTALLATION), and each attached board appears in Wireshark's capture list as "Ubertooth One \fB<serial>\fR"\. Running the calls below by hand is how to find out why a board does not appear there\.
.P
The script needs ubertooth\-util(1) and ubertooth\-btle(1) on the \fBPATH\fR Wireshark starts it with: it lists the attached boards with the first and captures with the second\. The board needs the \fBbluetooth_rxtx\fR firmware, as it does for ubertooth\-btle(1) itself\.
.SH "INSTALLATION"
The script is not installed by \fBmake install\fR: Wireshark loads extcap programs from its own directory, which differs between Wireshark versions and platforms\. Copy or link the script into the personal extcap directory Wireshark names under Help, About Wireshark, Folders ("Personal Extcap path"); Wireshark 4\.4 and later look in a \fBwireshark\fR subdirectory of it:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ln \-s "$PWD/host/python/extcap/btle\-extcap\.py" <that path>/
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
The script must be executable\. Restart Wireshark, or refresh the interface list, and the boards appear\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
Wireshark supplies all of these itself\. They are listed because the script is worth running by hand when a board does not appear in Wireshark\.
.TP
\fB\-\-extcap\-interfaces\fR
one \fBinterface\fR line per attached board, named \fBubertooth<serial>\fR\. A board whose serial cannot be read is left out, since it has no name to offer, and the boards that could be named are still listed\. No board attached is an empty listing and exit 0; a listing ubertooth\-util(1) failed on exits 1 and reports what it said, whether or not it named a board
.TP
\fB\-\-extcap\-interface=ubertooth<serial>\fR
the board every other call applies to\. The serial is passed to \fBubertooth\-btle \-U\fR, so a board keeps its interface across a reboot or a replug, which an enumeration index does not\. An \fBubertooth<index>\fR saved by an older version still works, because \fBubertooth\-btle \-U\fR reads an all\-digit value as an index
.TP
\fB\-\-extcap\-dlts\fR
the one link type the capture produces, \fBDLT_BLUETOOTH_LE_LL_WITH_PHDR\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-extcap\-config\fR
the configurable arguments, which is the advertising channel
.TP
\fB\-\-capture \-\-fifo=PATH [\-\-channel=37|38|39]\fR
run ubertooth\-btle(1) on the selected board and write the capture into \fBPATH\fR (default channel 37)\. Wireshark names the fifo it opened; \fB\-\-fifo=\-\fR is standard output, for a capture run by hand\. That one is refused when standard output would not take the capture; see DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
\fB\-\-extcap\-version[=<version>]\fR
the calling Wireshark's version, appended to the \fB\-\-extcap\-interfaces\fR call by Wireshark 2\.9 and later\. Accepted and ignored: nothing here behaves differently for different versions\. The value may be written \fB\-\-extcap\-version=5\.0\fR, \fB\-\-extcap\-version 5\.0\fR or left off altogether
.TP
\fB\-\-extcap\-capture\-filter=<filter>\fR
a capture filter the user typed\. ubertooth\-btle(1) captures every advertising packet it sees and cannot apply one, so a non\-empty filter is reported invalid; see CAPTURE FILTERS
.TP
\fB\-V\fR
print the version and end the call: the host tools, then the firmware version and build of each attached board\. The same answer every other tool gives, including with no board attached; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS\. The script answers it by running ubertooth\-util(1) \fB\-V\fR, which it needs on the \fBPATH\fR as it does for the listing, and reports one that is not there as \fBerror: could not run ubertooth\-util: No such file or directory\fR\. The script has no \fB\-U\fR, so every attached board is reported, and the call ends before \fB\-\-extcap\-interface\fR is read\. Wireshark never makes this call
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
print the usage line on standard output and exit 0\. It takes effect once the whole command line has parsed, so a stray word or an unknown option is still reported
.P
\fB\-V\fR and \fB\-h\fR both end the call, and both answer on standard output, so a call with both prints both: the version first and the usage line last, whichever order they were written in\. The status is then \fB\-V\fR's, so a board that could not be read makes a call carrying \fB\-h\fR exit 1, with the usage line printed all the same\. Either option ends the call ahead of every other one, so \fB\-\-extcap\-interfaces \-h\fR prints the usage line and lists nothing\.
.P
Everything else Wireshark can pass, it passes only to a program whose listing advertised the feature behind it: \fB\-\-extcap\-reload\-option\fR to one with a reloadable argument, \fB\-\-extcap\-control\-in\fR and \fB\-\-extcap\-control\-out\fR to one with toolbar controls, \fB\-\-extcap\-config\-option\-name\fR and \fB\-\-extcap\-config\-option\-value\fR to one with a configurable table value\. This script advertises none of them, so those options can only arrive by mistake and are refused like any other unknown option\.
.SH "CAPTURE FILTERS"
Wireshark verifies a capture filter by calling the program with the interface and \fB\-\-extcap\-capture\-filter=<filter>\fR and no \fB\-\-capture\fR, and shows on the filter box whatever that call writes to standard output\.
.P
This interface applies no capture filter, so a non\-empty filter is answered with \fBthis interface does not apply capture filters; use a display filter\fR on standard output, and Wireshark shows that sentence in the filter box in red\. An empty filter, which is what Wireshark verifies when the box is empty, is good: the answer is an empty standard output\. Either way the call exits 0, because a non\-zero exit would make Wireshark discard the answer and report "Unable to check capture filter" instead of showing it\. The one exception is a verdict that could not be printed at all, which is \fBerror: standard output has gone away\fR and exit 1: there is nothing left for Wireshark to discard\. See DIAGNOSTICS\.
.P
A filter given alongside \fB\-\-capture\fR does not stop the capture: it starts anyway, with a \fBwarning:\fR line repeating the same sentence\.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
Check that the boards Wireshark would list are the ones attached:
.IP "" 4
.nf
btle\-extcap\.py \-\-extcap\-interfaces
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Capture on one board by hand, writing PCAP to standard output:
.IP "" 4
.nf
btle\-extcap\.py \-\-extcap\-interface=ubertooth<serial> \-\-capture \-\-fifo=\- \e
    \-\-channel=38 > advertising\.pcap
.fi
.IP "" 0
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
Errors and warnings go to standard error, as does the usage line after a bare call (\fBerror: nothing to do: give \-\-extcap\-interfaces, \-\-extcap\-dlts, \-\-extcap\-config or \-\-capture\fR, then the usage line)\. The script's own standard output carries only the listings and the capture\-filter answer, the capture data going to the fifo instead, and nothing when a call fails\.
.P
The ubertooth\-btle(1) a \fB\-\-capture\fR call runs writes its own decode of every packet to its standard output, which is dropped: nobody asked this call for it, and 8 seconds of ambient advertising is some 560 kB\. Its standard error is the script's, so a ubertooth\-btle(1) that fails still says why\. Wireshark keeps the first 1024 bytes of the capture call's standard error and shows them for the interface when the call fails\.
.P
That 1024 bytes is there for a real failure to use: ubertooth\-btle(1) sums up the packets whose data could not be read on the stream the decode went to, which this call drops, so a good capture no longer ends in \fBError from extcap pipe\fR and a board unplugged mid\-capture reaches the user\. See ubertooth\-btle(1), DIAGNOSTICS\.
.P
\fB\-\-fifo=\-\fR is the one capture whose data is on standard output: it is the \fB\-\fR every file option here takes, for a capture run by hand rather than one Wireshark asked for, and ubertooth\-btle(1) writes the PCAP data there and no decode\.
.P
A command line the script will not take is refused before ubertooth\-btle(1) runs, so nothing is written to the fifo: \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-\-list\-dlts'\fR, in the wording every other tool here uses for the same mistake, with \fBerror: option '\-\-fifo' requires a value\fR for a missing value and, for the two mistakes only a long option can make, \fBerror: option '\-\-capture' takes no value\fR and \fBerror: option '\-\-extcap\-c' is short for more than one option\fR\. A word that is not an option or the value of one is \fBerror: unexpected argument 'extra'\fR, since no call here takes an operand\. The rest are \fBerror: \-\-extcap\-interface must be ubertooth<serial>, as \-\-extcap\-interfaces lists it\fR, \fBerror: \-\-capture needs \-\-fifo=PATH\fR and \fBerror: advertising channel index must be 37\-39 (got 40)\fR, which is ubertooth\-btle(1)'s own wording for the same mistake\. A call with no operation in it gives the same \fBerror: nothing to do: \|\.\|\.\|\.\fR line as a bare call, whether or not it named an interface, and a lone \fB\-\-\fR is such a call; the interface is read, and the first of those three lines given, only for a call that did ask for an operation\. Each is that one line: the usage line follows only a bare \fBbtle\-extcap\.py\fR, and \fB\-h\fR prints it on standard output\.
.P
Whatever ubertooth\-util(1) and ubertooth\-btle(1) write to standard error is passed on, including \fBerror: no Ubertooth found with serial matching '<serial>'\fR for a board that has been unplugged since the listing\. Wireshark shows what the \fB\-\-capture\fR call wrote there for the interface when the call fails; for the other calls it reads only standard output, so those lines land on Wireshark's own error output, which is also what someone running the call by hand sees\.
.P
The one line held back is ubertooth\-util(1)'s \fBerror: no Ubertooth devices found\fR, and only when it is the sole error it reported about an empty listing, since the empty listing says the same thing\. Alongside any other \fBerror:\fR line it is passed on with the rest\.
.P
A tool that is not on the \fBPATH\fR Wireshark started the script with is reported as \fBerror: could not run ubertooth\-util: No such file or directory\fR, and one that is there but cannot be executed names that reason instead (\fBPermission denied\fR)\. A ubertooth\-util(1) that failed without saying why is reported as \fBerror: ubertooth\-util failed (exit status 3)\fR, since a failure the user is told about has to name itself\.
.P
A \fB\-V\fR with no standard output to print the answer to, which is what \fBbtle\-extcap\.py \-V >&\-\fR leaves, is \fBerror: standard output has gone away\fR and exit 1\. ubertooth\-util(1) is not started there: with that descriptor closed, the first file it opened would take its number and the answer would be written into that\. A descriptor open for reading only (\fBbtle\-extcap\.py \-V 1<&0\fR) is the same line and status, and starts no ubertooth\-util(1) either: the answer is the child's to print, and nothing but the child's own exit status would say it was refused\.
.P
An answer whose standard output has gone is that same line and exit 1, whichever call was answering: the usage line \fB\-h\fR prints, the interface listing, the DLT, the configuration, and a capture filter's verdict\. It covers both descriptors a shell can leave, one closed before the script started (\fBbtle\-extcap\.py \-h >&\-\fR) and one open for reading only (\fBbtle\-extcap\.py \-h 1<&0\fR), where the line reaches the buffer and the flush is what fails\. A \fB\-V \-h\fR that loses both halves of its answer says it once\. An answer with nothing in it loses nothing and is not reported: an interface listing with no board to name, and the empty answer that says a capture filter is good, still exit 0 with that stream gone\.
.P
A \fB\-\-fifo=\-\fR capture is the same line and exit 1, and starts no ubertooth\-btle(1): the capture is the child's to write to that stream, so with it closed (\fBbtle\-extcap\.py \-\-capture \-\-fifo=\- >&\-\fR) the first file the child opened would take its number and the capture would be written into that, and with it open for reading only every write would fail\. A capture named by Wireshark goes to a real fifo and is unaffected\.
.P
With standard error gone, the \fBerror:\fR lines above are lost rather than moved: none of them falls back to standard output, which Wireshark reads as the answer to the call\. A standard error open for reading only (\fBbtle\-extcap\.py \-\-bogus 2<&0\fR), closed (\fBbtle\-extcap\.py \-\-bogus 2>&\-\fR), or a pipe whose reader has left (\fBbtle\-extcap\.py \-\-bogus 2>&1 | true\fR) all go the same way\. The exit status is then all that is left to say what happened, and it is the script's own: it used to be 120, from the flush the interpreter makes as it exits\. A child the script starts is handed the bin for that stream rather than a closed descriptor, for the reason the capture above is refused one\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
The call either did what was asked, which is 0, or it did not, which is 1\. Nothing else is reported: Wireshark needs no more than non\-zero for a failed capture, so the script does not pass ubertooth\-btle(1)'s own status through or report the signal that killed it\.
.TP
\fB0\fR
the call did what was asked\. For \fB\-\-capture\fR that includes a capture that was asked to stop, either way it ends: SIGTERM or SIGINT arrives and the script asks ubertooth\-btle(1) to stop with a SIGTERM, or ubertooth\-btle(1) dies of SIGPIPE because nothing is reading the fifo any more\. It also includes the capture\-filter verification call, whose answer is its standard output and not its status, unless that answer could not be printed; see CAPTURE FILTERS
.TP
\fB1\fR
a usage error, a board that could not be opened, a tool that could not be run, a ubertooth\-btle(1) that failed or was killed, or any other failure\. That includes a partial listing: if \fB\-\-extcap\-interfaces\fR could not open one board it exits 1 even though it named the others, with the reason on standard error\. Wireshark throws away everything a call that failed printed, so its capture list is then without the boards that were fine until that board is freed or unplugged\. The one case that is not a failure is no board attached: the empty listing answers that, and exits 0
